data.content:
Here's the full migration. Your code has three patterns that must change:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (true)` in `RunLoop` | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| **Blocking call interruption** | `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` |
| **Thread.Interrupt + Thread.Abort** | `Stop()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var token = _cts.Token;
        var handles = new[] { _newEvent, token.WaitHandle };

        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // WaitAny returns index 1 if the token is cancelled,
            // waking the thread without Thread.Interrupt
            int index = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);
            if (index == 1)
                break; // cancellation signalled

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();          // wakes WaitAny via token.WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);    // wait for graceful exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback — cooperative cancellation
        // guarantees the loop exits at the next checkpoint
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

1. **`Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 6+. The loop now voluntarily exits when the token is signalled.

2. **`Thread.Interrupt()` removed** — It only worked on threads in `WaitSleepJoin` state and is obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9). `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` achieves the same wake-up without it.

3. **`ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` → `WaitHandle.WaitAny`** — The original `WaitOne()` could not be interrupted by cancellation. `WaitAny` unblocks on *either* a new event or cancellation.

4. **Added `IDisposable`** — `CancellationTokenSource` leaks timers/callbacks if not disposed.

5. **Defensive `Dequeue`** — The original code threw if `WaitOne` returned spuriously or if multiple signals drained the queue. The guard prevents that race.

## Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| **No forcible kill exists** | If `Handle(item)` hangs forever, `Stop()` will block at `Join`. Design `Handle` to accept and check a `CancellationToken` too. |
| **`WaitOne` after `Reset` race** | The original code had a bug: if two items were enqueued rapidly, `Reset` could clear the signal before the second was processed. Consider `BlockingCollection<T>` for production use. |
| **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** | If you later make `Handle` async and token-aware, let `OCE` propagate up to `RunLoop` — don't catch-and-ignore it. |
| **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** | Forgetting this leaks internal kernel objects. Always wrap in `using` or call `Dispose`. |

## Production Alternative

For new .NET 8 code, `Channel<T>` is the idiomatic replacement for this entire class:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _task;

    public void Start() =>
        _task = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            Handle(item);
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) =>
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();
        _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
        if (_task is not null)
            await _task;
    }

    public void Dispose() => _cts.Dispose();
}
```

This eliminates the manual thread, lock, queue, and wait handle entirely. `Channel<T>` handles all synchronization internally and integrates natively with `CancellationToken`.
